BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 461.77 Tm (The landmine every practitioner names is the due-on-sale clause. Tim Talks Real Estate spells out the mechanics:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 446.55 Tm (almost every regular mortgage carries the clause, a wrap almost always transfers the property while the original loan is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 431.32 Tm (still open, and the lender can demand the whole balance, not the monthly payment but the whole thing, the day they) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 416.10 Tm (find out [Dklhw3jMfVo @ 00:03:16]. Brian Gibbons calls it the main problem with wraps and adds the buyer-side trap:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 400.87 Tm (the seller collects the buyer's payment and then never pays the first mortgage [9rVA292DHlY @ 00:01:49]. Gibbons) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 385.65 Tm (handles both by putting the property in a land trust and routing the buyer's money to a third-party payee that pays the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 370.42 Tm (mortgage first and sends the rest to the seller. Scott Horne runs the same defense through a loan servicer: buyer pays) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 355.20 Tm (servicer, servicer pays the underlying lender first, seller gets whatever is left. Horne calls it the industry's fraud) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 339.97 Tm (preventer, because a later default can then only mean the buyer missed, not that the seller pocketed the money) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 324.75 Tm ([VDXOvtqzbZk @ 00:07:47]. Chris Goff wants an escrow company coordinating the monthly payment for the same) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 309.52 Tm (reason, flagging the risk in just paying the seller and hoping the seller is making the payment [X_UODDIMvFs @) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 294.30 Tm (00:06:51].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 279.07 Tm (The second failure mode is the buyer simply stopping. PrepAgent's Sam-and-Bill example flips from win-win to 'not so) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 263.85 Tm (fast': if Bill stops paying, Sam cannot pay the existing mortgage, the bank gets angry and it becomes a big issue) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 248.62 Tm ([uHpaHL4qN84 @ 00:02:45]. Shop Rates calls buyer default the biggest risk point because the seller stays legally) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 233.40 Tm (responsible for the original mortgage, telling sellers to choose their buyers wisely and buyers to make sure the seller is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 218.17 Tm (financially stable [Z_X2-JRfBYs @ 00:01:26]. Ron LeGrand puts the credit cost on the table: selling on a wrap means) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 202.95 Tm (nobody pays off the underlying debt, so a personally guaranteed loan stays the seller's responsibility for as long as it) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 187.72 Tm (takes the buyer to cash them out. With guaranteed debt in play, LeGrand says to be more selective on who goes in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 172.50 Tm (house, or even sell at retail price right now and get that debt out of your life [OuQxeQu2HJA @ 00:05:15]. Brian) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 157.27 Tm (Gibbons adds the closing-table bleed: title transfers and a new mortgage or trust deed gets recorded, attorney fees stack) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 142.05 Tm (on top, and if the seller is getting no money down they may refuse to bring cash, so the investor can wind up paying all) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 126.82 Tm (the costs just to get the deal done [9rVA292DHlY @ 00:04:50].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 111.60 Tm (Then come the economics checks. Maximilian Dier says the place people get in trouble is overpaying: buy at $300,000) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 96.37 Tm (on a house only worth $270,000, wrap it out at $280,000 telling yourself you will make it up over time, and if the buyer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 81.15 Tm (refinances or pays the loan off in full, you are screwed. Dier's rule is to buy under market value with a low interest rate) Tj ET
